      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Acute coronary dissection is an uncommon event, usually not related to typical coronary risk factors, usually in women, and usually diagnosed at autopsy. This report describes a young woman with extensive left anterior descending coronary artery (LAD) dissection, refused for intervention since there was no lesion to angioplasty and no artery to bypass. A long arteriotomy was made, removing under direct vision all of the torn and dissected tissue, just as would be done for extensive LAD endarterectomy. A vein was split and attached to reconstruct the artery. Normal left ventricular function was restored."},
